why 1 0 SIMILAR_MOTION The tune leaps up a fourth to C and all voices rise into the same C minor chord, the bass by an octave. Only the spacing changes, not the harmony.
why 1 0 DIRECT The tune leaps G–C while the bass leaps an octave on C, both within one chord. Nothing new is hidden, since the harmony stays the same.
why 2 1/4 OVERLAP The tenor leaps up to F, above where the alto was, while the alto leaps up to A♭. They move up together.
why 2 1/4 LEAPS The tenor rises B–C and leaps to F. The tritone B–F spans two chords, so it is not heard as one interval.
why 2 3/4 SIMILAR_MOTION At the fermata every voice drops within the same G major chord, the bass by an octave. Only the spacing changes, not the harmony.
why 2 3/4 DIRECT The tune falls B–G while the bass drops an octave on G, within one chord. Nothing new is hidden.
why 2 3/4 LEAPS Alto (A♭–G–D) and tenor (F–D–B) fall through the dominant chord's notes into the fermata. Their tritones span several notes and two chords.
why 6 0 OVERLAP The bass leaps up a fourth to C while the tenor steps B–C, meeting in unison.
why 7 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION A cadence on E♭ (III): soprano F–E♭, alto A♭–G, tenor D–B♭ and the bass B♭–E♭. All the resolutions point down.
why 8 7/8 LEAPS The tenor falls E♭–D–C and skips to A within the diminished chord. Its ends form a tritone, spread over a line.
why 9 0 TENDENCY The tenor leaves C by a skip down to A and then rises to D, the fifth of V, moving through the diminished chord's notes instead of falling by step.
why 9 1/4 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Tenor and bass move from D/G to E♭/A♭ a fifth apart, with the tenor's run E♭–F in between. Bach treats the run as separating them; strict exercises still count them.
why 9 1/2 SPACING The alto holds a high C while the tenor drops to A and climbs back B–C. The gap lasts two sixteenths.
why 9 5/8 SPACING The tenor is climbing back toward the alto; the gap closes on the next note.
why 9 3/4 TENDENCY The alto's C leaps down to G, the root of V, instead of stepping to B. The tenor already has B, so the alto doubles the root.
why 10 0 SIMILAR_MOTION The piece ends on C major, a Picardy third. Every voice falls into it: the alto's F to E, the tenor's B to G and the bass to C.
